Lemma Engineering Rules
1. Technology

Use:

Next.js
React
TypeScript
Vercel-compatible server architecture
KaTeX
appropriate syntax highlighting
modern CSS architecture or Tailwind where appropriate

Do not introduce dependencies without a clear reason.

2. Architecture

Separate concerns between:

UI
content rendering
article data
authentication
authorization
search
media/assets
review workflow
publishing

Do not create giant components containing unrelated functionality.

Prefer reusable components and strongly typed interfaces.

3. Vercel

The production application will be deployed on Vercel.

Do not assume Vercel itself provides every backend capability.

Database, authentication, and object storage should be treated as separate infrastructure decisions unless a Vercel-native solution is explicitly selected.

Do not hard-code a provider before it has been chosen.

4. Development Process

Before making substantial changes:

Inspect the existing repository.
Understand the current architecture.
Check existing components and utilities.
Check the relevant project rules.
Check relevant decision records.
Identify potential conflicts.
Explain major architectural changes before implementing them.
5. Incremental Development

Implement features in small coherent phases.

Do not build the entire application in a single pass.

Do not rewrite unrelated working code.

Do not replace an existing architecture simply because another approach is personally preferred.

6. Type Safety

Prefer explicit TypeScript types.

Avoid unnecessary:

any

Do not suppress TypeScript errors merely to make the build pass.

Fix the underlying problem.

7. Security

Treat authentication, authorization, file uploads, article publishing, and user-generated content as security-sensitive.

Never trust client-side authorization.

Never expose secrets to client-side code.

Never execute arbitrary user-provided code on the production application without a deliberately designed sandbox.

8. Media

V1 article graphs are uploaded images.

Supported graph/image formats:

PNG
JPEG/JPG

Do not implement arbitrary Python execution for graph generation unless explicitly requested as a later feature.

9. Code Blocks

Use a proper syntax-highlighting solution.

Code blocks should support:

language labels
syntax highlighting
copy button
responsive horizontal scrolling

Do not store syntax-highlighted HTML as article content if it can be generated at render time.

10. Testing

After meaningful changes:

run the appropriate type checks
run linting
run tests where available
verify the affected pages
inspect responsive behaviour when UI changes are made

Do not declare a feature complete solely because the code compiles.

11. AI Model Responsibilities

Use the strongest reasoning model available for:

architecture
security-sensitive decisions
database design
search architecture
difficult debugging
major refactoring

Use a strong coding model for:

normal implementation
React components
styling
editor features
routine backend work

Use fast models for:

small mechanical edits
simple refactors
repetitive changes
straightforward documentation

Do not repeatedly redesign the architecture using different models.

12. Change Discipline

Before modifying an important architectural decision:

inspect the relevant decision record
determine whether it is still valid
explain why the change is necessary
update the decision record if the decision genuinely changes

Do not silently create architectural drift.